    "candidate_text": "For each \\(x\\in[0,1)\\), we have \\(x^n\\to0\\), while \\(f_n(1)=1\\) for every \\(n\\). Thus \\((f_n)\\) converges pointwise to\n\\[\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&0\\le x<1,\\\\1,&x=1.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nTake \\(\\varepsilon=\\tfrac12\\). For every \\(N\\in\\mathbb Z_+\\), set \\(n=N\\) and \\(x=2^{-1/N}\\). Then \\(x\\in[0,1)\\), so \\(f(x)=0\\), whereas\n\\[\n|f_n(x)-f(x)|=x^N=\\frac12.\n\\]\nHence, for every \\(N\\), there exist \\(n\\ge N\\) and \\(x\\in[0,1]\\) such that \\(|f_n(x)-f(x)|\\ge\\varepsilon\\). Therefore \\((f_n)\\) does not converge uniformly to its pointwise limit. Since any uniform limit must also be the pointwise limit, the sequence does not converge uniformly to any function on \\([0,1]\\).",
